{"name":"Green Balloon","hex":"#80c4a9","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"},{"term":"balloon","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An inflatable buoyant object, often (but not necessarily) round and flexible.","Such an object as a child’s toy or party decoration."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To increase or expand rapidly.","To go up or voyage in a balloon."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/balloon"}],"entries":[{"title":"Zielony Balonik","blocks":[{"text":"Zielony Balonik was a popular literary cabaret founded in Kraków by the local poets, writers and artists during the final years of the Partitions of Poland. The venue was a gourmet restaurant of Apolinary J. Michalik called the Michalik's Den. The cabaret was founded in 1905 and ran regularly until 1912."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Jama_Michalika.jpg/330px-Jama_Michalika.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zielony_Balonik","wikidata":"Q8071650"},{"title":"Green Balloon Club","blocks":[{"text":"Green Balloon Club is a British children's factual television program that started on CBeebies on 20 June 2008."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/a/a3/Green_Balloon_Club_Logo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green_Balloon_Club","wikidata":"Q5602305"},{"title":"Balloon","blocks":[{"text":"A balloon is a flexible membrane bag that can be inflated with a gas, such as helium, hydrogen, nitrous oxide, oxygen, or air. For special purposes, balloons can be filled with smoke, liquid water, granular media, or light sources. Modern-day balloons are made from materials such as rubber, polychloroprene, or a nylon fabric, and can come in many different colors. Some early balloons were made of dried animal bladders, such as the pig bladder. Some balloons are used for decoration or entertainment, while others have practical purposes such as meteorology, medical treatment, military defense, or transportation. A balloon's properties, including its low density and low cost, have led to a wide range of applications."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Congrats_bqt.jpg/330px-Congrats_bqt.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Balloon","wikidata":"Q9706"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Giant_Balloon_Nutcracker_Figures_for_Rays_Game.jpg/500px-Giant_Balloon_Nutcracker_Figures_for_Rays_Game.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Giant_Balloon_Nutcracker_Figures_for_Rays_Game.jpg/1280px-Giant_Balloon_Nutcracker_Figures_for_Rays_Game.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Giant_Balloon_Nutcracker_Figures_for_Rays_Game.jpg/500px-Giant_Balloon_Nutcracker_Figures_for_Rays_Game.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Decorations made of balloons with a combination of stacking and twisting techniques showcasing the deco-twisting style.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGiant_Balloon_Nutcracker_Figures_for_Rays_Game.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Parada_Gay_em_Sampa.jpg/500px-Parada_Gay_em_Sampa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Parada_Gay_em_Sampa.jpg/500px-Parada_Gay_em_Sampa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Decorative rainbow colored arches made of party balloons used at the pride parade in São Paulo, Brazil.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AParada_Gay_em_Sampa.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Dopey_Balloon_Twisting_Table_Centerpiece_for_Rays_Draft_Room.jpg/500px-Dopey_Balloon_Twisting_Table_Centerpiece_for_Rays_Draft_Room.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Dopey_Balloon_Twisting_Table_Ce